WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.984
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.924 --> 00:00:06.090
DAN MCGRATH: Awesome, thanks
for coming in everyone.

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:09.540
Quick introduction, as you
come into the Cloud Datastore

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:10.860
one on one session.

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:14.340
I'm Dan McGrath, I'm the product
lead for Cloud Datastore.

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:15.840
We're lucky to
have today with us

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:18.630
another speaker, Phil
Keslin from Niantic Labs,

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:21.600
who will come up and give us a
little talk about how Niantic

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:23.587
Labs uses Cloud Datastore.

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:29.360
So, what I want you to
leave here with today,

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:33.110
is an understanding of
what Cloud Datastore is,

00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:36.170
and in what situations
you would use it.

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:38.210
I also want you to be
able to walk out of here,

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:40.520
and just have an
idea of how you would

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:43.880
be able to talk to it from your
programming language of choice,

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:47.270
and also see how
you can integrate

00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:50.090
into the other parts of
the Google Cloud platform

00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:52.640
as part of a holistic solution.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:56.060
And as I just mentioned before,
once we get all that out

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:59.120
of the way, Phil will come up on
stage and give you a real world

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:00.650
example --

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:03.080
literally a real
world example --

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:08.010
of how they used it in
the launch of Pokemon GO.

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:10.680
But before we get
into all that, I

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:13.770
wanted to give you some
background context of tasks

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:15.840
that you typically
have to do when

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:18.090
you're running a
database, particularly

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:19.360
a database at scale.

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:25.460
So there is a lot of
background infrastructure work

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:27.132
that you need to set up.

00:01:27.132 --> 00:01:29.090
Most of you would already
have this set up now,

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:30.923
but it is something you
need to think about.

00:01:30.923 --> 00:01:33.380
Everything from shipping
in your infrastructure

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:35.660
from the back of the
truck, to setting up

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:38.720
the air conditioning and the
network on your rack and stack.

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:41.700
And then, as your
server is running,

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:44.030
you need to think about
ongoing maintenance,

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:46.790
and eventually upgrades
to that system.

00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:48.680
All this is time that
you could be spending

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:51.860
on working on your application,
rather than your application's

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:54.880
infrastructure.

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:57.760
Once you've got all that
groundwork set up, then you've

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:01.330
got the joy of picking
out an operating system,

00:02:01.330 --> 00:02:04.480
and working out your
upgrade and patch schedule,

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:07.840
which, hopefully, you are doing
as new security vulnerabilities

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:10.419
are detected, you're upgrading
your operating system

00:02:10.419 --> 00:02:13.480
continually, and working out
when your scheduled downtime is

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:14.350
to make that happen.

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:19.000
Once that's out of the
way, you can finally

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:21.280
start thinking
about your database,

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:23.440
and working out which
database version works

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:27.070
with which operating system
version that you have.

00:02:27.070 --> 00:02:30.742
Looking at the patch schedule
that you have for the database,

00:02:30.742 --> 00:02:32.200
and when you want
to roll that out,

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:34.120
and whether you can
roll patches out online,

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:36.250
or whether you need
to take your system

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:38.780
offline to apply those patches.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:40.990
And so, at this point,
now you have your database

00:02:40.990 --> 00:02:43.120
up and running on
an operating system

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:45.040
that you're maintaining,
on a hardware

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:47.950
stack that you are maintaining.

00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:49.990
You haven't quite
finished there.

00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:52.960
Now, the last task that
you have is thinking

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:54.740
about high availability.

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:56.920
How do you make sure
your system will continue

00:02:56.920 --> 00:02:59.170
running when you have
a regional outage,

00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:01.390
and how do you
scale that system?

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:04.570
As your application grows
or gets unexpected traction,

00:03:04.570 --> 00:03:10.370
what are you going to do to add
new capacity to your database?

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:12.080
Oh, and I forgot
to mention, there's

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:15.020
also monitoring of
this entire stack.

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:18.080
Everything from what's happening
with your air con system--

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:20.580
is your operating system
appropriately patched?

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:22.100
Are there operating
system alerts

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:26.690
being issued, the database
itself, is out of date?

00:03:26.690 --> 00:03:29.030
All that sort of stuff
is things that you're

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:31.700
going to be carrying, or
someone within your organization

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:33.150
is going to have a pager for.

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:39.450
So, with that context
in mind, I want

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:41.070
to let you know
I'm not promising

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:42.930
that Cloud Datastore
is going to solve

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:45.840
all these problems for
you, but for the right use

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:49.590
case and the right workload,
it will put you in a position

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:52.260
where you don't really need
to think about the database

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:54.330
that you're running,
you're just able to use it

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:55.710
as part of your application.

00:03:59.810 --> 00:04:02.730
Now, Cloud Datastore is just
one of the database offerings

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:07.380
we have within our managed
database and storage portfolio.

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:12.880
It really, as a managed No-SQL
database for user-facing data,

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:17.620
sits in that
non-relational space.

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:20.640
The other option
is Cloud Bigtable.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:22.440
If you have
relational workloads,

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:25.200
we have the option of
Cloud SQL or Cloud Spanner.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:27.210
We also have everything
from object storage

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:28.950
persistent disc--

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:34.151
Google Cloud Storage is the
object storage I mentioned.

00:04:34.151 --> 00:04:35.650
And then if you're
using App Engine,

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:38.175
you do have that hosted
memcache solution.

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:42.946
All of this can be
pretty overwhelming,

00:04:42.946 --> 00:04:44.820
trying to work out what
is the right database

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:46.080
solution for you.

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:48.390
So I wanted to give you
a bit of a cheat sheet

00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:51.510
of some quick questions you can
ask yourself to work out what

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:54.000
is the right database for me.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:56.310
Perhaps it's not
Cloud Datastore.

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:58.270
For me, hopefully it is.

00:04:58.270 --> 00:05:00.630
So, the first question
I want you to ask

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:04.080
is are you working with
semi-structured or structured

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:04.710
data?

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:06.251
I think this is a
really quick pass--

00:05:06.251 --> 00:05:09.390
you'd be be surprised how
many people would end up using

00:05:09.390 --> 00:05:11.010
the wrong solution here--

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:12.630
but if you've got
unstructured data,

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:14.940
really look towards
cloud storage.

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:17.010
And if you do have
unstructured data,

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:20.430
don't try to force
it into a database.

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:22.770
The next question is
really understanding

00:05:22.770 --> 00:05:24.660
what is your primary use case.

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:27.940
Is it transactional data,
or is it analytical data?

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:30.240
If you're really
looking to do analytics

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:32.250
as your primary
workload, you should

00:05:32.250 --> 00:05:35.520
be looking at something
like Bigtable, for internet

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:38.070
of things, Device
Data, or BigQuery

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:42.240
for that more traditional
data warehouse at scales.

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:43.620
The last question
you really need

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:46.440
to think about before you
get to Cloud Datastore

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:49.470
is, is it relational data?

00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:51.930
If you are doing a
lot of complex joins,

00:05:51.930 --> 00:05:55.020
you should be looking at
either Cloud SQL for that lift

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:57.960
and shift model of your
traditional MySQL or postgres

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:01.260
instances, or if you're looking
for that massively horizontally

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:05.115
scalable SQL database, Cloud
Spanner is the right solution.

00:06:08.420 --> 00:06:09.940
If your answer to
those questions

00:06:09.940 --> 00:06:15.870
are no, then Cloud Datastore
is a really good, hands off,

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:19.650
no ops, non-relational data
source that you can use.

00:06:22.712 --> 00:06:25.170
Once you've got that out of
the way and you're thinking OK,

00:06:25.170 --> 00:06:27.711
Cloud Datastore looks like it
could be a pretty good solution

00:06:27.711 --> 00:06:28.680
for what I'm doing--

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:30.960
I'm doing a mobile
or web application,

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:32.832
I don't really
have DBAs on staff,

00:06:32.832 --> 00:06:34.290
and I just want to
launch something

00:06:34.290 --> 00:06:36.600
that I don't have
to think about--

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:39.630
Cloud Datastore can really
fill that fast fully managed

00:06:39.630 --> 00:06:44.670
solution that will literally
scale with zero downtime.

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:47.430
It will do everything
from serving your data

00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:50.040
from a single machine,
and then, as you scale up,

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:52.440
will go to tens of
thousands of machines.

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:55.770
We have many customers who
run multi-millions of requests

00:06:55.770 --> 00:06:58.230
per second against the
system, without having

00:06:58.230 --> 00:07:01.560
to worry about configuring
the replication system,

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:05.180
or worry about how many
nodes they have to add to it.

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:08.880
To give you an idea of the
scale that it operates at,

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:12.690
early last year, in
2016, we were already

00:07:12.690 --> 00:07:16.300
serving 15 trillion
requests per month.

00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:19.680
So you can do some quick
math and work out how many

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:22.069
requests per second that is.

00:07:22.069 --> 00:07:23.610
The last thing I
just want to mention

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:27.930
is this is not an
untested solution.

00:07:27.930 --> 00:07:31.140
It's been used by our customers
in production for eight years,

00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:34.020
and is also used, both
internally and externally,

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:35.010
by Google software.

00:07:39.050 --> 00:07:41.630
Some example use cases,
these are the sort

00:07:41.630 --> 00:07:43.760
of typical workloads
that we see people

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:46.070
using Cloud Datastore for.

00:07:46.070 --> 00:07:49.160
The first one, and this is
by far the biggest use cases,

00:07:49.160 --> 00:07:52.352
is user profiles on mobile
and web applications.

00:07:52.352 --> 00:07:53.810
If you want to
store something that

00:07:53.810 --> 00:07:57.380
has a flexible schema, that
you can continually iterate

00:07:57.380 --> 00:08:00.380
on what type of data you're
storing about the user,

00:08:00.380 --> 00:08:02.870
without having to worry
about schema changes,

00:08:02.870 --> 00:08:05.659
Cloud Datastore can be a really
flexible solution for that,

00:08:05.659 --> 00:08:07.450
since we don't force
you to have a schema--

00:08:07.450 --> 00:08:10.415
you could just add new
fields as you need them.

00:08:10.415 --> 00:08:13.040
The next one is-- and I'll talk
about some of the functionality

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:14.870
that helps with this later on--

00:08:14.870 --> 00:08:17.750
is catalogs and any sort
of hierarchical data

00:08:17.750 --> 00:08:20.480
where you want to logically
structure stuff together.

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:22.880
So, if you're thinking about
doing review sites where

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:25.420
you have items that you
want to have reviews on.

00:08:25.420 --> 00:08:28.160
And that is a good example
of hierarchical data

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:30.490
that you can store with us.

00:08:30.490 --> 00:08:34.280
And the last one is
durable state for games.

00:08:34.280 --> 00:08:36.380
Working out what
inventory players have,

00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:38.159
where items are
around the world,

00:08:38.159 --> 00:08:40.830
you'll typically have a lot
of that cached in memory,

00:08:40.830 --> 00:08:43.159
so you can do very
low latency serving,

00:08:43.159 --> 00:08:45.950
but because, whether
you're are using pods

00:08:45.950 --> 00:08:48.620
in Container Engine,
or traditional VMs,

00:08:48.620 --> 00:08:50.300
these things will
eventually crash,

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:53.933
and you don't want to
have that state lost.

00:08:53.933 --> 00:08:57.790
[NO SPEECH]

00:08:57.790 --> 00:09:00.490
Now, with some of the features
that you get with Cloud

00:09:00.490 --> 00:09:03.700
Datastore, the first one
I wanted to point out,

00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:05.560
even though we're a
no-SQL database --

00:09:05.560 --> 00:09:07.540
we handled
non-relational data --

00:09:07.540 --> 00:09:09.970
we do have some of those
features you'd traditionally

00:09:09.970 --> 00:09:11.690
want within a database.

00:09:11.690 --> 00:09:14.800
So the first one is
multi-document transactions.

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:17.860
You can do transactions
across multiple documents

00:09:17.860 --> 00:09:18.680
in the database.

00:09:18.680 --> 00:09:20.530
It's not just read
and write, and then

00:09:20.530 --> 00:09:23.130
hope for ACID compliance.

00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:25.510
And the second one is, we
have really good support

00:09:25.510 --> 00:09:28.150
for secondary and
composite indexes.

00:09:28.150 --> 00:09:29.800
We have some
limitations in there

00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:33.550
that are designed to make sure
you design your system in a way

00:09:33.550 --> 00:09:35.200
that scales, but
we will allow you

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:38.230
to do multiple equality
filters and inequality

00:09:38.230 --> 00:09:40.604
filters on those properties.

00:09:40.604 --> 00:09:42.270
And the last one--
and this is something

00:09:42.270 --> 00:09:43.990
I think we don't
talk about enough--

00:09:43.990 --> 00:09:46.180
is it is automatically
replicated

00:09:46.180 --> 00:09:48.550
across multiple zones
in multiple regions

00:09:48.550 --> 00:09:50.800
without you having
to manage anything.

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:54.230
It is literally a one click
set up when you set it up,

00:09:54.230 --> 00:09:56.860
and then you don't have
to think about that again.

00:09:56.860 --> 00:09:59.110
And the last one, as you
would expect from most Google

00:09:59.110 --> 00:10:01.850
databases, everything
is encrypted at rest

00:10:01.850 --> 00:10:03.950
and in transit, as well.

00:10:03.950 --> 00:10:06.250
And this is using the
same encryption technology

00:10:06.250 --> 00:10:08.820
Google uses for all our
products, like Gmail.

00:10:13.109 --> 00:10:15.150
And then to quickly finish
off with the features,

00:10:15.150 --> 00:10:18.540
I wanted to give you an idea
of some of the basic building

00:10:18.540 --> 00:10:21.150
blocks that you have to
create your application.

00:10:21.150 --> 00:10:24.090
So you have the laundry
list of data types

00:10:24.090 --> 00:10:26.310
that you'd be looking for
everything from booleans,

00:10:26.310 --> 00:10:28.050
integers, down to blobs.

00:10:28.050 --> 00:10:30.030
You have some slightly
more advanced stuff

00:10:30.030 --> 00:10:33.030
you can use, with arrays
and embedded entities,

00:10:33.030 --> 00:10:35.010
to use a non-Google term.

00:10:35.010 --> 00:10:37.530
Embedded entities you
can think of as just maps

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:40.210
that you store within your data.

00:10:40.210 --> 00:10:41.910
And up here, I
just wanted to give

00:10:41.910 --> 00:10:44.850
a quick example of hierarchies,
and how you would have that

00:10:44.850 --> 00:10:45.780
stored.

00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:48.990
So you may have something
like a user in your system,

00:10:48.990 --> 00:10:51.690
and then under that user you
would have a bunch of orders

00:10:51.690 --> 00:10:53.700
that they've done, and
perhaps a list of billing

00:10:53.700 --> 00:10:56.400
accounts for that
particular customer.

00:10:56.400 --> 00:10:58.740
This hierarchy, and
how it structures,

00:10:58.740 --> 00:11:00.720
has implications
on whether you have

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:03.570
strong or eventual consistency,
and the right throughput

00:11:03.570 --> 00:11:05.221
for your data.

00:11:05.221 --> 00:11:06.720
If you want to know
more about that,

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:08.190
there was a technical
talk we gave

00:11:08.190 --> 00:11:10.479
yesterday called
"Building Scalable

00:11:10.479 --> 00:11:11.520
Apps on Cloud Datastore."

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:13.200
So you can look
at that on YouTube

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:15.360
and it will tell you more
about the details of how

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:16.710
that affects consistency.

00:11:19.810 --> 00:11:22.330
When I originally set up to do
this talk, someone asked me,

00:11:22.330 --> 00:11:25.950
could I do a presentation
on setting up replication,

00:11:25.950 --> 00:11:28.180
multi-region replication?

00:11:28.180 --> 00:11:31.530
And I decided it shouldn't
be a talk by itself,

00:11:31.530 --> 00:11:34.600
and I'll just put it in
here as a couple of slides.

00:11:34.600 --> 00:11:37.680
The first step to setting
up multi-region replication

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:41.500
within Cloud Datastore
is going into our console

00:11:41.500 --> 00:11:46.570
after you've created a project,
and clicking on Create Entity.

00:11:46.570 --> 00:11:49.720
Right now, we only have
one database per project.

00:11:49.720 --> 00:11:52.240
We have IAM permissions
you can assign to it.

00:11:52.240 --> 00:11:54.760
So you can assign either
ownership role or user

00:11:54.760 --> 00:11:58.040
role, which is read and
write, or read only,

00:11:58.040 --> 00:11:59.920
to the database
itself, so you can

00:11:59.920 --> 00:12:03.010
control who has
access to it compared

00:12:03.010 --> 00:12:05.050
to the rest of the project.

00:12:05.050 --> 00:12:09.130
This is also used as the
default database for App Engine.

00:12:09.130 --> 00:12:10.690
So if you're using
App Engine, this

00:12:10.690 --> 00:12:14.500
will also provision the
default database it will use.

00:12:14.500 --> 00:12:17.920
So, step one, click
on Create Entity.

00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:20.590
Step two it's going
to ask you where

00:12:20.590 --> 00:12:23.380
you want to put your instance.

00:12:23.380 --> 00:12:26.710
Now, this is where you get to
choose between multi-region,

00:12:26.710 --> 00:12:30.160
which is that multi-zone
multi-region replication,

00:12:30.160 --> 00:12:33.400
or just regional replication.

00:12:33.400 --> 00:12:35.920
The second choice, as
part of this drop-down,

00:12:35.920 --> 00:12:39.970
is about where do you want
your data to be located.

00:12:39.970 --> 00:12:43.520
You can pick North America
with US central or US East 1,

00:12:43.520 --> 00:12:47.140
you can pick Europe with our
EUS multi-region instance,

00:12:47.140 --> 00:12:50.160
or you can pick Tokyo, Japan
for a regional instance.

00:12:55.331 --> 00:12:55.830
That's it.

00:12:55.830 --> 00:12:58.290
That's why I didn't make it
as a separate presentation.

00:12:58.290 --> 00:13:01.260
The moment you make that choice
of where you want your data

00:13:01.260 --> 00:13:05.790
located, you are running a
multi-region replicated service

00:13:05.790 --> 00:13:08.850
that is running five
replicas across three

00:13:08.850 --> 00:13:12.550
independent network fabrics.

00:13:12.550 --> 00:13:14.830
So let's talk about
that a little bit more,

00:13:14.830 --> 00:13:16.840
and what does it really mean.

00:13:16.840 --> 00:13:19.090
I've already mentioned you
can pick multiple locations

00:13:19.090 --> 00:13:22.340
to have your data stored
in North America, Europe,

00:13:22.340 --> 00:13:24.740
and Asia.

00:13:24.740 --> 00:13:27.450
We also have points of presence
located around the world.

00:13:27.450 --> 00:13:30.100
Now, a point of presence
is an egress and ingress

00:13:30.100 --> 00:13:33.550
point to our network that
goes onto the private fiber

00:13:33.550 --> 00:13:36.550
optic we have, so you can
have low latency access

00:13:36.550 --> 00:13:39.370
to wherever your computer or
database resources are located,

00:13:39.370 --> 00:13:42.670
from anywhere around the world.

00:13:42.670 --> 00:13:46.510
When you pick between
regional or multi-region,

00:13:46.510 --> 00:13:49.650
the two different options
of replication we have,

00:13:49.650 --> 00:13:53.590
it does change what the
SLA is that we offer.

00:13:53.590 --> 00:13:56.320
If you pick a
multi-regional instance

00:13:56.320 --> 00:13:59.410
it's three and a half nines; if
you pick a regional instance,

00:13:59.410 --> 00:14:00.760
it's three nines.

00:14:00.760 --> 00:14:03.070
Now, in practice,
our systems actually

00:14:03.070 --> 00:14:06.280
operate above those levels,
but that's the guarantee

00:14:06.280 --> 00:14:09.610
that we give for those systems
because regional outages do

00:14:09.610 --> 00:14:10.420
happen.

00:14:10.420 --> 00:14:13.150
So if you really can't afford
to have your application go down

00:14:13.150 --> 00:14:14.950
when a region goes
down, we highly

00:14:14.950 --> 00:14:19.010
recommend you pick the
multi-region solution.

00:14:19.010 --> 00:14:21.130
The other difference
to keep in mind,

00:14:21.130 --> 00:14:24.910
and think about why you might
pick a regional location--

00:14:24.910 --> 00:14:28.070
one is location.

00:14:28.070 --> 00:14:30.730
If all your user base
is, say, in Japan,

00:14:30.730 --> 00:14:32.650
then perhaps you want
to have the Tokyo, Japan

00:14:32.650 --> 00:14:35.860
instance, because then it's
co-located with your users.

00:14:35.860 --> 00:14:38.140
The last one is write latency.

00:14:38.140 --> 00:14:41.140
Because we synchronously
replicate across regions,

00:14:41.140 --> 00:14:43.870
you will have slightly
higher write latency

00:14:43.870 --> 00:14:45.035
on a multi-region instance.

00:14:50.660 --> 00:14:53.270
Now, the last thing
that I'm going

00:14:53.270 --> 00:14:57.170
to talk about when we look
at regional replication

00:14:57.170 --> 00:15:01.430
and multi-region replication
is this quick visualization

00:15:01.430 --> 00:15:03.440
on the system, just
so you can understand

00:15:03.440 --> 00:15:05.300
how we're structuring
this under the covers.

00:15:05.300 --> 00:15:08.540
And this is a very
simplified version.

00:15:08.540 --> 00:15:12.980
This is an example of US Central
where region one is Oklahoma,

00:15:12.980 --> 00:15:18.630
region two is Iowa, and region
three is South Carolina.

00:15:18.630 --> 00:15:22.190
So we do two full
replicas in Oklahoma,

00:15:22.190 --> 00:15:26.420
two full replicas
of your data in Iowa

00:15:26.420 --> 00:15:28.580
running in separate
zones, and then

00:15:28.580 --> 00:15:31.850
we have a third one that we call
a witness that essentially acts

00:15:31.850 --> 00:15:33.770
as a tiebreaker if
one of these regions

00:15:33.770 --> 00:15:37.114
go down that's located
in South Carolina.

00:15:37.114 --> 00:15:38.780
Now, all of these are
connected together

00:15:38.780 --> 00:15:42.030
by three fully independent
fiber optic networks.

00:15:42.030 --> 00:15:43.980
So we can have a network
down for maintenance,

00:15:43.980 --> 00:15:46.190
we can have a backhoe
go through another one,

00:15:46.190 --> 00:15:54.750
and we can continue to serve all
your traffic at full capacity

00:15:54.750 --> 00:15:57.502
Now, I won't go too much into
the weeds with consistency,

00:15:57.502 --> 00:15:59.460
but I think this is an
important thing to note.

00:15:59.460 --> 00:16:03.000
Depending on how you
work with your data,

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:05.340
we support both
eventual consistency

00:16:05.340 --> 00:16:08.650
and strong consistency
within the system.

00:16:08.650 --> 00:16:10.770
Now, the easy way
to think about this

00:16:10.770 --> 00:16:13.140
is if you're using us
like a key value store.

00:16:13.140 --> 00:16:16.560
So, if you're just doing
gets, look ups, and writes,

00:16:16.560 --> 00:16:19.290
we're strongly consistent
because we will synchronously

00:16:19.290 --> 00:16:23.250
replicate that data
across our network.

00:16:23.250 --> 00:16:26.670
You get eventual consistency
when you're using our indexes,

00:16:26.670 --> 00:16:29.610
because the indexes themselves
are eventually consistent.

00:16:29.610 --> 00:16:32.460
And that's how we sustained
throughput regardless

00:16:32.460 --> 00:16:34.680
of how many indexes you write.

00:16:34.680 --> 00:16:37.620
There is one caveat
to that, and that's

00:16:37.620 --> 00:16:40.890
if you're doing a thing that
we call an ancestor query, that

00:16:40.890 --> 00:16:42.810
is also strongly consistent.

00:16:42.810 --> 00:16:44.310
And ancestor queries
are when you're

00:16:44.310 --> 00:16:46.650
querying within one of
those single hierarchies,

00:16:46.650 --> 00:16:48.210
so single entity groups.

00:16:48.210 --> 00:16:49.830
If you co-locate
your data we can

00:16:49.830 --> 00:16:53.100
guarantee a strongly consistent
query in that particular case.

00:16:57.540 --> 00:17:00.090
To give you a little bit of
background on why this matters

00:17:00.090 --> 00:17:02.310
when you're designing
your system,

00:17:02.310 --> 00:17:05.040
there are many use cases
where you absolutely

00:17:05.040 --> 00:17:06.990
require strong
consistency, and there

00:17:06.990 --> 00:17:10.170
are some use cases where
you would prefer scalability

00:17:10.170 --> 00:17:13.950
and write throughput than having
to worry about slowing down

00:17:13.950 --> 00:17:15.900
for strong consistency.

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:17.520
So the example I
wanted to give here

00:17:17.520 --> 00:17:20.260
is if you're doing a
social networking site,

00:17:20.260 --> 00:17:22.260
it would be really
unfortunate if you

00:17:22.260 --> 00:17:26.130
have a post about someone's
surprise birthday party

00:17:26.130 --> 00:17:28.329
that you delete, and
then you add that friend

00:17:28.329 --> 00:17:30.720
to your social network,
if they can still

00:17:30.720 --> 00:17:33.660
see that post about their
surprise birthday party because

00:17:33.660 --> 00:17:35.400
of eventual consistency.

00:17:35.400 --> 00:17:38.610
That's a pretty basic example of
why you want strong consistency

00:17:38.610 --> 00:17:40.740
in situations like that.

00:17:40.740 --> 00:17:43.980
Strong consistency is
all about making sure

00:17:43.980 --> 00:17:46.740
that multiple people
and systems get

00:17:46.740 --> 00:17:51.990
a consistent view of the
database at the same time.

00:17:51.990 --> 00:17:55.590
Now there are definitely places
where eventual consistency is

00:17:55.590 --> 00:17:58.050
completely applicable as well.

00:17:58.050 --> 00:18:00.150
A quick example here is,
if you are doing a census

00:18:00.150 --> 00:18:03.240
population of an
area, it can be very

00:18:03.240 --> 00:18:06.720
hard to do a strongly consistent
count of everyone in, say,

00:18:06.720 --> 00:18:10.140
the United States, because
you've got to work out when do

00:18:10.140 --> 00:18:13.230
you count a birth, and there
may be minutes difference

00:18:13.230 --> 00:18:13.970
between them.

00:18:13.970 --> 00:18:15.840
Now, if you're off
by a couple of people

00:18:15.840 --> 00:18:18.000
when you're looking
at 300 million,

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:19.870
it's not such a big deal.

00:18:19.870 --> 00:18:21.990
Or if you've got a
gaming review site,

00:18:21.990 --> 00:18:24.840
if you miss the last
update from one user that

00:18:24.840 --> 00:18:27.570
happened five seconds ago
when you displayed the list,

00:18:27.570 --> 00:18:28.800
it's not such a big deal.

00:18:32.640 --> 00:18:36.090
So with some of the fundamentals
out of the way there,

00:18:36.090 --> 00:18:38.570
I wanted to quickly go through
some of the multiple ways

00:18:38.570 --> 00:18:40.850
that you can talk
to Cloud Datastore.

00:18:40.850 --> 00:18:43.580
So we went GA with our
new client libraries

00:18:43.580 --> 00:18:45.860
in our new API last
year, that allows you

00:18:45.860 --> 00:18:48.320
to access it cross-platform.

00:18:48.320 --> 00:18:50.420
Previously, we were
only part of App Engine,

00:18:50.420 --> 00:18:51.980
and now we have a
way to access it,

00:18:51.980 --> 00:18:55.040
whether you're calling in
from your on prem solution,

00:18:55.040 --> 00:19:00.500
from App Engine, Container
Engine, and Compute Engine.

00:19:00.500 --> 00:19:02.630
The languages that
we support natively,

00:19:02.630 --> 00:19:05.060
using our Google
Cloud client libraries

00:19:05.060 --> 00:19:12.320
are Java, Python, Go Node.js,
Ruby, C Sharp, and now

00:19:12.320 --> 00:19:14.000
we have PHP as well.

00:19:14.000 --> 00:19:17.210
These all have a very
consistent interface,

00:19:17.210 --> 00:19:19.100
so when you're switching
between languages,

00:19:19.100 --> 00:19:22.220
it should still feel
like you're interacting

00:19:22.220 --> 00:19:25.400
with the same database.

00:19:25.400 --> 00:19:29.120
If we don't support a language
that you're interested

00:19:29.120 --> 00:19:32.480
in, and typically I'll get
questions around things like

00:19:32.480 --> 00:19:37.370
C++ or embedded devices, we
do have a RESTful API that we

00:19:37.370 --> 00:19:40.820
document, so as long as
you can do HTTPS calls,

00:19:40.820 --> 00:19:42.430
and you know how
to handle oauths,

00:19:42.430 --> 00:19:45.125
you can connect to it from any
modern programming language.

00:19:48.980 --> 00:19:52.190
The last part of that
is we also support GRPC.

00:19:52.190 --> 00:19:55.430
So the underlying API
infrastructure is GRPC.

00:19:55.430 --> 00:19:58.640
So if you want to do that
streaming, low latency request,

00:19:58.640 --> 00:20:01.190
you can also use that.

00:20:01.190 --> 00:20:03.470
I did mention before
that we were originally

00:20:03.470 --> 00:20:07.130
part of App Engine, and we
do have a rich ecosystem

00:20:07.130 --> 00:20:10.100
of SDKs or frameworks that
you can use that we've

00:20:10.100 --> 00:20:11.940
developed over the years.

00:20:11.940 --> 00:20:14.780
So, over the eight
years, we have

00:20:14.780 --> 00:20:17.480
had a variety of different
solutions you can do.

00:20:17.480 --> 00:20:20.300
But one I would point out for
Python, if you're using that,

00:20:20.300 --> 00:20:24.440
is something we call NDB or Next
DB, which is a full framework,

00:20:24.440 --> 00:20:27.110
integrates in with
memcache, task queues,

00:20:27.110 --> 00:20:29.400
as well as Datastore.

00:20:29.400 --> 00:20:32.252
We also have some
third-party ORMs

00:20:32.252 --> 00:20:33.710
that people have
developed that are

00:20:33.710 --> 00:20:35.930
really popular within
our customer base.

00:20:35.930 --> 00:20:37.820
So if you are
using Java, there's

00:20:37.820 --> 00:20:39.950
something called Objectify
that a lot of people

00:20:39.950 --> 00:20:43.490
use on App Engine, and there is
also a client library for PHP.

00:20:49.200 --> 00:20:51.420
OK, so we've talked about
programming languages.

00:20:51.420 --> 00:20:53.029
We've talked about
strong consistency.

00:20:53.029 --> 00:20:54.570
We've talked about
the basic building

00:20:54.570 --> 00:20:56.640
blocks, and the
underlying replication

00:20:56.640 --> 00:20:58.600
that you get with the system.

00:20:58.600 --> 00:21:00.360
Let's look outside
of Datastore now,

00:21:00.360 --> 00:21:01.890
and talk about some
of the products

00:21:01.890 --> 00:21:02.940
that we integrate with.

00:21:05.770 --> 00:21:08.830
Everything from data warehousing
to export your data if you want

00:21:08.830 --> 00:21:12.260
to do complex standard
SQL queries, to data flow,

00:21:12.260 --> 00:21:15.550
if you want to rapidly fan out
across the data either writing

00:21:15.550 --> 00:21:17.520
it in or reading
it out to integrate

00:21:17.520 --> 00:21:20.500
into other parts of the
system is available.

00:21:20.500 --> 00:21:22.090
And, as I mentioned
before, you can

00:21:22.090 --> 00:21:26.670
call us from any of the
compute services that we have.

00:21:26.670 --> 00:21:30.990
Let's quickly look at how we
integrate with Cloud storage.

00:21:30.990 --> 00:21:33.360
We have a backup,
or an export tool

00:21:33.360 --> 00:21:37.040
that you can call that will
do a full dump of your data.

00:21:37.040 --> 00:21:40.440
You can optionally give
it a specific subset

00:21:40.440 --> 00:21:43.230
by either kind, which is
sort of like table name,

00:21:43.230 --> 00:21:47.110
or name space, which is a way
you can separate your data.

00:21:47.110 --> 00:21:48.780
We will write that
into an export format

00:21:48.780 --> 00:21:49.904
that you can use elsewhere.

00:21:49.904 --> 00:21:51.330
But I just want
to point out it is

00:21:51.330 --> 00:21:55.050
in a LevelDB format, which
is an open source database

00:21:55.050 --> 00:21:57.426
that we released in 2011.

00:21:57.426 --> 00:21:58.800
So you can write
any of your code

00:21:58.800 --> 00:22:00.540
to read and write
into this format

00:22:00.540 --> 00:22:04.860
if you want to do your
own fancy thing with it.

00:22:04.860 --> 00:22:08.040
When you have an export
in Cloud Storage,

00:22:08.040 --> 00:22:10.590
within BigQuery, you
can use that as a target

00:22:10.590 --> 00:22:13.200
to ingest into your system.

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:15.750
This is how you get your
data warehouse experience.

00:22:15.750 --> 00:22:18.270
A lot of people we find do
daily dumps of their data

00:22:18.270 --> 00:22:21.200
so they have a full history
that they compare to the system.

00:22:25.570 --> 00:22:28.540
Cloud Dataflow, which is Apache
Beam-- it's our execution

00:22:28.540 --> 00:22:31.180
environment for Apache Beam--
is a really good way, if you

00:22:31.180 --> 00:22:34.240
want to do those
massive fan out reads.

00:22:34.240 --> 00:22:37.120
You can use this as
both a source and a sink

00:22:37.120 --> 00:22:42.050
within the system, and you
can auto-scale the system.

00:22:42.050 --> 00:22:44.470
So, depending on whether you
want to get it done really

00:22:44.470 --> 00:22:47.320
quickly, or you want to
save money using sustained

00:22:47.320 --> 00:22:49.600
discounts-- because it
runs on Compute Engine--

00:22:49.600 --> 00:22:53.210
you can control how
fast this happens.

00:22:53.210 --> 00:22:55.850
We typically see people
using Cloud Dataflow

00:22:55.850 --> 00:22:58.220
to do live ingestion
into BigQuery query,

00:22:58.220 --> 00:23:00.590
rather than going
through that full dump.

00:23:00.590 --> 00:23:02.480
But we also have
people integrating it

00:23:02.480 --> 00:23:05.900
into things like Pub/Sub, so
they can push messages out

00:23:05.900 --> 00:23:10.310
into other parts of
their infrastructure.

00:23:10.310 --> 00:23:12.730
And the last one I wanted
to quickly point out

00:23:12.730 --> 00:23:15.100
here is Stackdriver monitoring.

00:23:15.100 --> 00:23:17.050
We are integrated
into Stackdriver,

00:23:17.050 --> 00:23:19.300
and there are four key
metrics you can look at.

00:23:19.300 --> 00:23:21.040
You can look at the
overall request rate

00:23:21.040 --> 00:23:22.780
that you have in your system.

00:23:22.780 --> 00:23:27.190
You can look at the average
and standard deviation on read

00:23:27.190 --> 00:23:29.490
and write size so you
can see if anything's

00:23:29.490 --> 00:23:30.860
changing in your system.

00:23:30.860 --> 00:23:32.730
And you can also see
what sort of fan-out

00:23:32.730 --> 00:23:34.637
you're having on index writes.

00:23:34.637 --> 00:23:36.970
And that's important just
because indexes are eventually

00:23:36.970 --> 00:23:37.930
consistent.

00:23:37.930 --> 00:23:41.530
That level of fan out
affects the time frame

00:23:41.530 --> 00:23:43.900
of eventual consistency.

00:23:43.900 --> 00:23:46.390
You can also set all
sorts of alerting on this,

00:23:46.390 --> 00:23:49.090
so if you want to get alerted
when your production traffic is

00:23:49.090 --> 00:23:51.190
spiking way above
what you expected,

00:23:51.190 --> 00:23:55.390
or if you want to set alerts
when it drops to zero, which

00:23:55.390 --> 00:23:57.650
indicates some part of
your system has crashed,

00:23:57.650 --> 00:24:01.160
you can also do that.

00:24:01.160 --> 00:24:04.090
So with this basic introduction
of Cloud Datastore out

00:24:04.090 --> 00:24:06.460
of the way, I want
to invite Phil up

00:24:06.460 --> 00:24:10.220
onto the stage to talk about how
they used it at Niantic Labs.

00:24:15.169 --> 00:24:16.210
PHIL KESLIN: Thanks, Dan.

00:24:19.610 --> 00:24:22.310
So, a little bit of
history about our company.

00:24:22.310 --> 00:24:25.660
We actually started
out inside of Google.

00:24:25.660 --> 00:24:27.640
Ingress, which is our
first product, which

00:24:27.640 --> 00:24:31.476
we launched in November of 2012,
was really built on App Engine.

00:24:31.476 --> 00:24:33.850
And at the time Datastore was
the only storage repository

00:24:33.850 --> 00:24:35.920
that we had available to us.

00:24:35.920 --> 00:24:37.840
Given the long
history of Ingress,

00:24:37.840 --> 00:24:42.310
which is four and a
half years old now,

00:24:42.310 --> 00:24:44.050
we have a great
deal of experience

00:24:44.050 --> 00:24:46.510
in how to effectively
utilize Datastore.

00:24:46.510 --> 00:24:51.560
And we utilized that knowledge
when we delivered Pokemon GO.

00:24:51.560 --> 00:24:53.890
Some more insights--
if you don't know much

00:24:53.890 --> 00:24:57.610
about these games, they are
real world MMOs, massively

00:24:57.610 --> 00:25:00.670
multiplayer online games,
and they are location based.

00:25:04.472 --> 00:25:05.930
Some of the key
features of this is

00:25:05.930 --> 00:25:08.466
we have to have a consistent
state for all of our players.

00:25:08.466 --> 00:25:10.340
All the players are
seeing the same gameboard

00:25:10.340 --> 00:25:11.360
in the real world.

00:25:11.360 --> 00:25:13.670
They see the same
gyms in Pokemon GO.

00:25:13.670 --> 00:25:16.160
In Ingress, they see
the same set of portals,

00:25:16.160 --> 00:25:17.810
they see interactions
with the portals.

00:25:17.810 --> 00:25:19.434
Actually, Ingress is
significantly more

00:25:19.434 --> 00:25:23.090
sophisticated than Pokemon
Go, as far as game play goes.

00:25:23.090 --> 00:25:25.220
So, we needed a solution
that would provide us

00:25:25.220 --> 00:25:29.960
with consistent state for
all the players, a mechanism

00:25:29.960 --> 00:25:33.980
to get scalable real world
mutations for both game

00:25:33.980 --> 00:25:35.960
and player state.

00:25:35.960 --> 00:25:39.590
Game updates are
done periodically.

00:25:39.590 --> 00:25:41.840
I don't know how many of
you actually play Ingress,

00:25:41.840 --> 00:25:44.090
but we add portals to the game.

00:25:44.090 --> 00:25:47.510
So, an ability to regenerate
portions of the game adding

00:25:47.510 --> 00:25:50.690
new features to it on the fly,
without taking the game down,

00:25:50.690 --> 00:25:53.787
is necessary, and for
that we use data flow.

00:25:53.787 --> 00:25:56.120
And then the last one is that
players are always moving,

00:25:56.120 --> 00:26:00.140
so you're always going to
have hotspots in your data.

00:26:00.140 --> 00:26:02.420
We needed the mechanism
and a scalable solution

00:26:02.420 --> 00:26:06.980
that would allow us to get
to that kind of gameplay.

00:26:06.980 --> 00:26:11.600
This is kind of a rough flow for
how our system is architected.

00:26:11.600 --> 00:26:14.840
We have the mobile client which
has a cache at a current game

00:26:14.840 --> 00:26:15.770
state.

00:26:15.770 --> 00:26:18.140
It's continually
querying the servers

00:26:18.140 --> 00:26:20.540
for more information
about the changes

00:26:20.540 --> 00:26:24.680
in the game, especially when
the players are moving around.

00:26:24.680 --> 00:26:27.260
It really only has a
small view of the world

00:26:27.260 --> 00:26:30.590
at any particular point in time.

00:26:30.590 --> 00:26:37.190
The server consists of a player
service and a back end spacial

00:26:37.190 --> 00:26:39.980
service, which
manages the world.

00:26:39.980 --> 00:26:42.210
Currently, it's actually
not running on this.

00:26:42.210 --> 00:26:47.240
At launch we were running on
tens of thousands of CPU cores

00:26:47.240 --> 00:26:51.110
with the Datastore
capable of handling

00:26:51.110 --> 00:26:52.397
the load that we were seeing.

00:26:52.397 --> 00:26:54.980
And I'll go a little bit through
that launch in just a minute.

00:26:58.350 --> 00:27:00.600
So, one of the reasons
we chose Datastore--

00:27:00.600 --> 00:27:02.250
and Dan highlighted this--

00:27:02.250 --> 00:27:06.660
is the engineering team for
Pokemon GO, on the server side,

00:27:06.660 --> 00:27:08.820
consisted of four people.

00:27:08.820 --> 00:27:11.030
And if you saw the
stack on the side,

00:27:11.030 --> 00:27:13.830
I have absolutely
no employee base

00:27:13.830 --> 00:27:17.174
to manage a data
center and a database.

00:27:17.174 --> 00:27:19.590
Somebody asked me the other
day, why don't you just deploy

00:27:19.590 --> 00:27:20.610
a Cassandra instance?

00:27:20.610 --> 00:27:22.590
I was like, well, because
I'd have to have two people.

00:27:22.590 --> 00:27:23.965
Half of my
engineering team would

00:27:23.965 --> 00:27:25.620
have to manage that
Cassandra instance.

00:27:25.620 --> 00:27:27.660
And they would have
to scale it once we

00:27:27.660 --> 00:27:31.900
launch, which would have been,
in my opinion, a nightmare.

00:27:31.900 --> 00:27:37.140
So we chose Datastore
because it offered us

00:27:37.140 --> 00:27:42.060
a very inexpensive solution
to our storage problems.

00:27:42.060 --> 00:27:44.030
The launch of Pokemon GO--

00:27:44.030 --> 00:27:47.070
I think everybody's kind of
aware of what happened there.

00:27:47.070 --> 00:27:52.770
We launched on the 5th of July
to New Zealand and Australia.

00:27:52.770 --> 00:27:56.950
And everybody was asking me
why did we pick those two.

00:27:56.950 --> 00:27:59.850
It's because Australia and New
Zealand we figured, at peak,

00:27:59.850 --> 00:28:03.600
would represent about 4%
of our total user base,

00:28:03.600 --> 00:28:09.210
and with the Play Store and
primarily the App Store,

00:28:09.210 --> 00:28:11.730
you can only do country
level deployments.

00:28:11.730 --> 00:28:13.950
So by doing country
level rollouts

00:28:13.950 --> 00:28:17.700
was the way that we controlled
traffic into our system.

00:28:17.700 --> 00:28:24.180
And at launch, on the
5th, within six hours

00:28:24.180 --> 00:28:28.380
we had exceeded our
expectations by two and a half

00:28:28.380 --> 00:28:33.910
x, using 4% of the
population that we expected.

00:28:33.910 --> 00:28:37.290
So, needless to say when we
pushed the button on the 6th

00:28:37.290 --> 00:28:39.960
to launch in the United
States, we were kind of

00:28:39.960 --> 00:28:40.980
on pins and needles.

00:28:40.980 --> 00:28:43.710
And this is the magical charm.

00:28:46.890 --> 00:28:51.030
When we originally
provisioned the system,

00:28:51.030 --> 00:28:52.680
we ran our load
tests, and we came up

00:28:52.680 --> 00:28:55.710
with what we thought were just
wildly unbelievable estimates

00:28:55.710 --> 00:28:59.310
for how much traffic
we would see.

00:28:59.310 --> 00:29:03.240
We used existing MMOs,
what their user base was,

00:29:03.240 --> 00:29:06.300
how big the traffic numbers
that they were seeing,

00:29:06.300 --> 00:29:09.270
and we said, OK, let's just take
that number and multiply by 10.

00:29:09.270 --> 00:29:10.910
That's a nice big number.

00:29:10.910 --> 00:29:11.910
And that's the red line.

00:29:14.156 --> 00:29:15.780
And then we modeled
that in our system,

00:29:15.780 --> 00:29:21.490
and and that's what we gave
to Dan as the amount of QPS

00:29:21.490 --> 00:29:23.760
that we would
throw at Datastore.

00:29:23.760 --> 00:29:27.564
The first day after we launched
New Zealand and Australia,

00:29:27.564 --> 00:29:28.980
we sat in the
meeting and we tried

00:29:28.980 --> 00:29:31.396
to figure out where we thought
this graph was going to be.

00:29:31.396 --> 00:29:36.840
And the number we came up with
was 30 times our expectations.

00:29:36.840 --> 00:29:38.760
In the end this chart doesn't--

00:29:38.760 --> 00:29:40.830
it still goes.

00:29:40.830 --> 00:29:46.020
We stopped it, but it actually
gets bigger than that.

00:29:46.020 --> 00:29:49.062
But it took us a
while to actually get

00:29:49.062 --> 00:29:50.520
all the resources
in place in order

00:29:50.520 --> 00:29:53.820
to enable the scaling
that happened.

00:29:53.820 --> 00:29:55.566
And, for that
first two weeks, we

00:29:55.566 --> 00:29:56.940
had a lot of bumps
along the way,

00:29:56.940 --> 00:29:59.010
and I'll go back to the slide.

00:29:59.010 --> 00:30:01.770
You can see the big gap
between the US and Germany.

00:30:01.770 --> 00:30:04.410
That big gap existed, because
we couldn't get everything

00:30:04.410 --> 00:30:06.930
in place in order to support
the additional users that we

00:30:06.930 --> 00:30:08.270
were going to throw at it.

00:30:08.270 --> 00:30:09.740
And then we went with Europe.

00:30:09.740 --> 00:30:13.740
We went kind of rolling push
through Europe, and then

00:30:13.740 --> 00:30:15.960
into Canada, and back
to the US, primarily

00:30:15.960 --> 00:30:18.112
because people have to sleep.

00:30:18.112 --> 00:30:19.320
At least we thought they did.

00:30:19.320 --> 00:30:20.195
They actually didn't.

00:30:23.800 --> 00:30:26.580
We were hopefully gaining
access to the diurnal cycles

00:30:26.580 --> 00:30:32.400
so that we could at least
smooth out our data usage.

00:30:32.400 --> 00:30:36.450
And then on the launch
in Japan on the 21st

00:30:36.450 --> 00:30:39.420
was really our mark.

00:30:39.420 --> 00:30:44.250
And we wanted it to
be absolutely smooth.

00:30:44.250 --> 00:30:46.580
And it did.

00:30:46.580 --> 00:30:49.090
We had zero problems
when we launched Japan.

00:30:52.380 --> 00:30:57.850
People have talked about,
as a small company--

00:30:57.850 --> 00:31:00.430
our entire company when
we launched Pokemon GO had

00:31:00.430 --> 00:31:03.029
probably 70 people in it --

00:31:03.029 --> 00:31:05.320
there's no way that I could
have managed a data center.

00:31:05.320 --> 00:31:06.778
There's no way I
could have managed

00:31:06.778 --> 00:31:10.570
the infrastructure necessary
to do all of this work.

00:31:10.570 --> 00:31:13.870
Having the Google team on
the other side, provisioning

00:31:13.870 --> 00:31:15.820
resources for us
to support the load

00:31:15.820 --> 00:31:18.220
that we were throwing
at the system,

00:31:18.220 --> 00:31:21.580
and having a robust enough
environment that just did not

00:31:21.580 --> 00:31:25.030
fall over, we had absolutely
no problems with Datastore

00:31:25.030 --> 00:31:26.920
during this time.

00:31:26.920 --> 00:31:29.830
We kept running out
of quota, but Dan

00:31:29.830 --> 00:31:31.820
took care of that for us.

00:31:31.820 --> 00:31:33.580
So this was kind of our launch.

00:31:35.967 --> 00:31:37.050
And that's pretty much it.

00:31:48.365 --> 00:31:50.880
DAN MCGRATH: Yeah, that
was definitely a fun ride.

00:31:50.880 --> 00:31:53.350
That was fun to
be involved with.

00:31:53.350 --> 00:31:55.680
It was also fun for a
lot of people on our team

00:31:55.680 --> 00:31:57.570
to see this sort of request.

00:31:57.570 --> 00:31:59.280
And when I'd come
with them and ask OK,

00:31:59.280 --> 00:32:02.140
and I think we need to add
this amount to the system,

00:32:02.140 --> 00:32:06.800
and they'd ask, did I get a
zero misplaced on that number?

00:32:06.800 --> 00:32:07.820
I was like no, no.

00:32:07.820 --> 00:32:12.150
We really need that, like, now.

00:32:12.150 --> 00:32:16.140
So, that, I think, is
a prototypical example

00:32:16.140 --> 00:32:17.940
of where we find
the real success

00:32:17.940 --> 00:32:21.210
stories with Cloud Datastore.

00:32:21.210 --> 00:32:22.710
You want to build
something, and you

00:32:22.710 --> 00:32:24.334
want to spend time
on your application,

00:32:24.334 --> 00:32:26.970
and you really can't afford
to either hire people or take

00:32:26.970 --> 00:32:30.660
people out of what they're
doing to manage this underlying

00:32:30.660 --> 00:32:31.560
infrastructure.

00:32:31.560 --> 00:32:33.476
You just want to be able
to use it and have it

00:32:33.476 --> 00:32:34.950
taken care of by someone else.

00:32:34.950 --> 00:32:39.030
And we are very happy to
take care of that for you.

00:32:39.030 --> 00:32:40.620
Having said that,
there are definitely

00:32:40.620 --> 00:32:42.870
cases where Cloud
Datastore really

00:32:42.870 --> 00:32:44.820
isn't the right
solution for you,

00:32:44.820 --> 00:32:46.770
and I will be the first
person to tell you,

00:32:46.770 --> 00:32:48.240
if you're talking to
me, whether you should

00:32:48.240 --> 00:32:49.980
be using Cloud
Datastore, or you should

00:32:49.980 --> 00:32:53.310
be using a different product.

00:32:53.310 --> 00:32:55.550
So, just to give you
a very high level

00:32:55.550 --> 00:32:59.000
view, towards the end of
the presentation here,

00:32:59.000 --> 00:33:02.300
is when you wouldn't want
to use Cloud Datastore,

00:33:02.300 --> 00:33:04.620
or when other solutions
might be better.

00:33:04.620 --> 00:33:07.040
Now, people definitely
use it in this way,

00:33:07.040 --> 00:33:09.230
and that's perfectly fine.

00:33:09.230 --> 00:33:10.880
There are going to
be gray areas when

00:33:10.880 --> 00:33:12.890
you work on your application.

00:33:12.890 --> 00:33:15.620
But this is a little bit
of a cheat sheet to think,

00:33:15.620 --> 00:33:19.520
am I using the right product
for this particular solution?

00:33:19.520 --> 00:33:22.040
And the first one that I
want you to think about

00:33:22.040 --> 00:33:25.970
is are you really looking
for that zero, one

00:33:25.970 --> 00:33:28.790
millisecond latency, maybe
even three milliseconds

00:33:28.790 --> 00:33:31.040
latency as acceptable for you.

00:33:31.040 --> 00:33:34.280
You really need to be looking
at some sort of in memory cache

00:33:34.280 --> 00:33:36.140
to sit in front
of your database.

00:33:36.140 --> 00:33:40.490
Very often we find people
within the database usergroup

00:33:40.490 --> 00:33:43.160
will have caches sitting in
front of their most latency

00:33:43.160 --> 00:33:47.650
sensitive data, and use it
like a write-through cache.

00:33:47.650 --> 00:33:51.050
An example here is you
could spin up your own redis

00:33:51.050 --> 00:33:53.420
instance, or memcache instance.

00:33:53.420 --> 00:33:55.250
Or if you happen to
be using App Engine,

00:33:55.250 --> 00:33:57.860
we provide a hosted
memcache solution

00:33:57.860 --> 00:33:59.660
that you can use
there if you want.

00:33:59.660 --> 00:34:02.612
Hands off in that
aspect as well.

00:34:02.612 --> 00:34:04.820
The second one-- and I talked
about this a little bit

00:34:04.820 --> 00:34:05.630
earlier--

00:34:05.630 --> 00:34:07.580
is if you're really
looking for analytics.

00:34:07.580 --> 00:34:09.409
Like if you're a business
department that's

00:34:09.409 --> 00:34:12.380
doing lots of reporting every
day, multiple times a day,

00:34:12.380 --> 00:34:13.440
on the data.

00:34:13.440 --> 00:34:15.469
We're not going to be a
great solution for that.

00:34:15.469 --> 00:34:19.130
We want to give you queries
that scale with your operation.

00:34:19.130 --> 00:34:21.170
We talk about it
in terms of scaling

00:34:21.170 --> 00:34:24.139
with the size of your results
set, rather than your data set.

00:34:24.139 --> 00:34:26.179
So if you query for
10 records, and you

00:34:26.179 --> 00:34:28.420
have 100 records
in your database,

00:34:28.420 --> 00:34:30.710
we return in the
same time whether you

00:34:30.710 --> 00:34:32.719
have 100 million records.

00:34:32.719 --> 00:34:34.880
Now, we can't provide
those sort of guarantees

00:34:34.880 --> 00:34:38.159
when we have complex
joins within the system.

00:34:38.159 --> 00:34:40.190
So, if you're looking
for something like that,

00:34:40.190 --> 00:34:42.620
take that export
experience I talked about

00:34:42.620 --> 00:34:45.920
before into Cloud storage, and
then bring that into BigQuery,

00:34:45.920 --> 00:34:47.750
and that will be much
more cost effective,

00:34:47.750 --> 00:34:50.480
and much more flexible, because
you can use that standard query

00:34:50.480 --> 00:34:51.929
language.

00:34:51.929 --> 00:34:54.190
The other one is,
if you're doing

00:34:54.190 --> 00:34:57.139
a more operational
database on your analytics,

00:34:57.139 --> 00:34:58.640
you're doing that
mixed workload.

00:34:58.640 --> 00:35:01.250
Where you've got transactions
and payment systems coming in

00:35:01.250 --> 00:35:04.280
and you're continually running
these joint statements,

00:35:04.280 --> 00:35:06.836
you probably don't want
to use BigQuery for that.

00:35:06.836 --> 00:35:08.210
What you want to
use is something

00:35:08.210 --> 00:35:10.700
like Cloud Spanner, which
is that horizontally

00:35:10.700 --> 00:35:14.330
scalable relational database.

00:35:14.330 --> 00:35:18.230
Now, Cloud Datastore can
do some very extreme loads,

00:35:18.230 --> 00:35:19.880
but there are some
situations when

00:35:19.880 --> 00:35:22.070
you're talking
about extreme loads

00:35:22.070 --> 00:35:24.950
where we're not going to have
the right trade-offs for you.

00:35:24.950 --> 00:35:26.690
We put transactions
on top of it.

00:35:26.690 --> 00:35:29.270
We have multi-region
Replication.

00:35:29.270 --> 00:35:32.900
If you're doing things like
Internet of things devices--

00:35:32.900 --> 00:35:35.090
if you're doing
a jumbo jet where

00:35:35.090 --> 00:35:37.130
you have sensors all
over it, and you're

00:35:37.130 --> 00:35:41.300
streaming 300 terabytes of
data at very quick rates,

00:35:41.300 --> 00:35:43.850
you want to look at
something like Bigtable

00:35:43.850 --> 00:35:48.380
that is specifically
designed as a zonal system

00:35:48.380 --> 00:35:52.010
to do this huge ingestion of,
essentially, a wide column

00:35:52.010 --> 00:35:53.970
store.

00:35:53.970 --> 00:35:56.510
The other one that
I commonly see

00:35:56.510 --> 00:36:00.770
is someone who wants that
pure lift and shift solution.

00:36:00.770 --> 00:36:04.430
They want to take the existing
solution they have on prem,

00:36:04.430 --> 00:36:05.970
and just move it to the Cloud.

00:36:05.970 --> 00:36:07.520
They don't want
much else to change.

00:36:07.520 --> 00:36:09.103
Maybe they don't
want to have to worry

00:36:09.103 --> 00:36:12.560
about the underlying hardware
infrastructure or OS updates,

00:36:12.560 --> 00:36:16.010
but they want the database
exactly as it is today.

00:36:16.010 --> 00:36:18.300
We're not going to be able
to provide that for you.

00:36:18.300 --> 00:36:20.990
So that's where you look at
things like Cloud SQL, which

00:36:20.990 --> 00:36:23.370
give you MySQL and
postgres support,

00:36:23.370 --> 00:36:26.510
so you can pretty much take
your application as it is today,

00:36:26.510 --> 00:36:28.070
and shift it into the Cloud.

00:36:31.670 --> 00:36:34.670
And before we go
into the Q&amp;A I want

00:36:34.670 --> 00:36:36.770
to leave you with
some next steps

00:36:36.770 --> 00:36:40.790
that you can do
after this session.

00:36:40.790 --> 00:36:42.760
The first one is, as
I mentioned before, it

00:36:42.760 --> 00:36:47.590
is super simple to get this
really complex data system up

00:36:47.590 --> 00:36:48.490
and running.

00:36:48.490 --> 00:36:53.470
Go to the console,
console.cloud.google.com,

00:36:53.470 --> 00:36:54.920
create your instance.

00:36:54.920 --> 00:36:56.740
You can actually
use it for free.

00:36:56.740 --> 00:36:59.380
I think we do
50,000 reads per day

00:36:59.380 --> 00:37:03.070
is a free tier, 20,000 writes,
20,000 deletes per day,

00:37:03.070 --> 00:37:04.630
and one gigabyte of storage.

00:37:04.630 --> 00:37:07.090
So you really can
play with it and not

00:37:07.090 --> 00:37:08.880
have to worry about
being charged.

00:37:08.880 --> 00:37:10.420
You can check out
our documentation.

00:37:10.420 --> 00:37:14.650
If you have technical questions,
myself and a bunch of engineers

00:37:14.650 --> 00:37:17.530
are quite frequently
on Stack Overflow.

00:37:17.530 --> 00:37:20.260
So you can always use the
Google Cloud Datastore tag,

00:37:20.260 --> 00:37:22.840
and one of us will most likely
see it and be able to give you

00:37:22.840 --> 00:37:24.460
a quick answer to your problem.

00:37:28.060 --> 00:37:33.510
[MUSIC PLAYING]

